From 0dca68417e69667559f4246f5dc5330c53452341 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 01:52:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] video library: what a playback found of the HDR of its video
 goes into the details it keeps

The library keeps the HDR of a video in the stream details of its file:
the HDR type of the video stream, the Dolby Vision profile in its HDR
detail ("5", "7FEL", "8.1"), and a second, virtual, video stream for the
HDR10+ or HLG of a Dolby Vision stream (DemuxerToStreamDetails()). A file
that is not scanned (a .strm, a disc, an add-on's stream) gets the details
that its first playback knew when it began, from the demuxer: no layer of
profile 7 before a picture told it, no HDR10+ (which only the pictures
carry), no virtual stream. A Blu-ray gets "dolbyvision" without a profile
and HDR10 for HDR10+ from its directory. A file scanned by an older Kodi
keeps "7", or no profile. And once a file has details, a playback changes
nothing of them.

When a playback ends, the player now puts on the item what it found of its
video (AttachPlayedVideo(): codec, size, HDR type, profile with the layer
of profile 7, HDR10+ seen in a decoded picture, the HLG base of 8.4, and,
for a stream that says no HDR, the transfer function of its first
picture), only where that video is the one of the item: not of a DVD, nor
of a Blu-ray playlist other than the one kept for the item
(CDVDInputStreamBluray::GetPlaylist()). CSaveFileState makes the details
it stores, or the ones the library has, say what that adds
(video/PlayedVideoHdr.h, utils/LibraryHdr.h), in Kodi's own columns and in
the form a scan writes, so a library shared with other Kodi 22 systems
reads them as it reads its own.

It only ever adds: the profile where the details have none, "7" ->
"7FEL"/"7MEL", the virtual HDR10+ or HLG stream of a Dolby Vision stream,
HDR10 -> HDR10+, and a type where the details have none only for the file
that played itself (not a .strm or a stream, which can be another video at
each playback) and not from an NFO. Details that the playback contradicts,
or of another video (codec or size), are kept as they are; a playback that
knows less changes nothing. The audio and subtitle streams are not
touched.

kodi.log says what was done: "CSaveFileState: the library's details of
the video now say ... (they said ...), as its playback found", or that
they are kept.
